Context: Göttingen, September 1832. This is the Gauss–Weber magnetometer and torsion work: the previous page is headed 'Reductionen beobachteter Schwingungen auf unendlich kleine', and the next page treats the torsion of the suspension. Every block on this page reduces an observed duration t of N swings to the duration for infinitely small arcs.

The formula behind every block (the derivation is mine; every number fits it):
- The period at angular amplitude α is T(α) ≈ T0(1 + α²/16).
- With mirror and scale, a scale amplitude a corresponds to α = a/(2r). Here r is the scale distance in scale divisions: r = 9333 for B, R = 9327 for A, as on the next page.
- The amplitude decays geometrically from a to b over the N swings. Put λ = ln(a/b) = d·ln10, with d = log a − log b.
- The mean of α² over the run is (α_a² − α_b²)/(2λ) = α_a·α_b·sinh λ/λ.
- Hence the correction is Δt = t · a·b·(sinh λ/λ) / (64 r²), and t0 = t − Δt.

How this appears in the logarithmic layout:
- The pair of logs is summed: log a + log b.
- log(sinh λ/λ) is added.
- The constant log(64r²) is subtracted. It is 9.74623 for r = 9333 and 9.74566 for R = 9327 (logs written in the −10 convention).
- log t is added; the antilog gives Δt.
- Δt is subtracted from t and the result divided by N.

Computing log(sinh λ/λ):
- When d is small, Gauss evidently uses the series 1 + λ²/6 + ….
- When d is large, he uses an exact side column: log(10^{2d} − 1) − d = log(10^d − 10^{−d}); then subtract log d; then add 9.33675 = log(1/(2 ln10)).

